1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:07,880
Listen.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,040
Practice.

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:09,880
Speak.

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:19,720
Okay, so have you ever like totally psyched yourself out before a job interview?

6
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:20,660
Oh, yeah, definitely.

7
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:21,840
Like, you know, you've got the skills.

8
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:22,220
Right.

9
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:26,340
But then you walk in and it's like all those carefully crafted answers just fly right out the window.

10
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:27,730
I've been there for sure.

11
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:31,680
Well, this deep dive is for anyone who's ever felt that pre-interview dread.

12
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:32,330
Definitely.

13
00:00:32,350 --> 00:00:40,390
Or even if you're not actively job searching right now, you know, just thinking ahead to like, you know, future career moves.

14
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:40,670
Yeah.

15
00:00:40,690 --> 00:00:41,470
Always be prepared.

16
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:41,730
Right.

17
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:42,410
Exactly.

18
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:43,180
Exactly.

19
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:48,980
So we're going to dig into this awesome guide all about like acing that job interview process.

20
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,120
It's really good.

21
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,790
It's full of like practical strategies that I think can help anyone stand out.

22
00:00:53,890 --> 00:00:54,670
So let's jump right in.

23
00:00:54,690 --> 00:00:55,230
Let's do it.

24
00:00:55,510 --> 00:01:01,590
One thing that really resonated with me in the guide was this idea that the interview process, it's like a game.

25
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:02,170
Hmm.

26
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:03,720
That's a great way to look at it.

27
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:06,530
You know, it has its own set of stages, its own rules.

28
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:07,290
Absolutely.

29
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:11,400
And like any game, the more you understand it, the better you can play it.

30
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:12,500
And the better you can win.

31
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:13,140
Exactly.

32
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,310
So let's talk about the first level, like pre-interview prep.

33
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:17,210
Yeah.

34
00:01:17,530 --> 00:01:19,950
I feel like everyone knows you should research the company.

35
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:20,710
Right.

36
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:21,950
But I'm guilty of this too.

37
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:22,690
Yeah.

38
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:26,100
You know, I often just skim the website and call it a day.

39
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:27,700
Yeah, I think a lot of people do that.

40
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:30,870
And it's definitely important to go beyond that surface level research.

41
00:01:30,890 --> 00:01:32,410
You know, like really dig deep.

42
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:34,130
So what would you say are some ways to do that?

43
00:01:34,150 --> 00:01:36,710
Like what are some of the things that people might be overlooking?

44
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:41,920
Well, I think a good place to start is by looking beyond the company's website.

45
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:50,430
OK. You know, like check out Glassdoor to get a feel for what current and former employees are saying about the company culture.

46
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:51,290
That's a good one.

47
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:51,770
Yeah.

48
00:01:51,970 --> 00:01:54,750
You know, like what's the work life balance really like?

49
00:01:54,910 --> 00:01:55,350
Yeah.

50
00:01:55,370 --> 00:01:56,230
Are people happy there?

51
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:57,280
Yeah, exactly.

52
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:02,390
And even looking at their financials, like are they investing in growth or cutting costs?

53
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:03,310
Those are great points.

54
00:02:03,330 --> 00:02:06,830
Because those details tell you a lot about the company's priorities.

55
00:02:06,850 --> 00:02:10,060
And it shows the interviewer that you've done your homework, like you're really interested.

56
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,940
OK, so you've done your research.

57
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:17,790
You're feeling prepared, but then you realize, wait, there are all these different types of interviews.

58
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:18,210
Right.

59
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:19,530
One-on-one panel group.

60
00:02:20,130 --> 00:02:23,080
Like how do you even begin to prepare for all of those?

61
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:25,140
Yeah, I know it can feel overwhelming.

62
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:25,560
It is.

63
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:30,770
But thinking back to that game analogy, each type of interview is like a different level.

64
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:31,700
I like that.

65
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:34,200
With its own unique challenges and strategies.

66
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:35,400
Okay, so let's break it down.

67
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:35,840
All right.

68
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:36,690
One-on-one.

69
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:37,010
All right.

70
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:40,490
So one-on-one is your classic boss battle, right?

71
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:40,970
Right.

72
00:02:40,990 --> 00:02:48,830
You've got to build rapport, make a connection with the interviewer, show them that you're someone they'd actually want to work with.

73
00:02:49,090 --> 00:02:49,410
Got it.

74
00:02:49,430 --> 00:02:51,110
So it's all about that personal connection.

75
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:51,710
Definitely.

76
00:02:51,790 --> 00:02:51,890
Okay.

77
00:02:51,910 --> 00:02:52,950
What about panel interviews?

78
00:02:53,590 --> 00:02:56,740
Panel interviews, those are more like fighting a team of bosses.

79
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:57,980
Oh, that sounds intimidating.

80
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:02,830
It can be a little bit, but the key here is to make sure you're addressing each person's concerns.

81
00:03:03,030 --> 00:03:03,290
Okay.

82
00:03:03,690 --> 00:03:07,540
Showing how your skills and experience align with the overall vision of the team.

83
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:11,670
So you kind of have to be aware of like the different perspectives in the room.

84
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:12,340
Exactly.

85
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:18,660
You've got to be adaptable and, you know, show that you can work effectively with a variety of personalities.

86
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:20,090
And then there's a group interview.

87
00:03:20,150 --> 00:03:20,950
The group interview.

88
00:03:20,970 --> 00:03:21,210
Yeah.

89
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:30,340
This is like those chaotic free for all battles, you know, where you're in a room with a bunch of other candidates and it can be a little intimidating.

90
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:30,700
Oh, yeah.

91
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:31,460
That sounds stressful.

92
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:31,740
Yeah.

93
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:37,280
But in this scenario, teamwork and communication, those become absolutely crucial.

94
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:40,470
So it's less about like outshining the competition.

95
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:40,830
Right.

96
00:03:41,290 --> 00:03:44,260
And more about like showcasing how well you can collaborate.

97
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:44,980
Exactly.

98
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:50,270
Because ultimately companies are looking for people who can work well with others.

99
00:03:50,290 --> 00:03:50,890
That makes sense.

100
00:03:50,910 --> 00:03:54,140
You know, contribute to a positive team environment.

101
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:57,190
Okay, so we've tackled the different types of interviews.

102
00:03:57,250 --> 00:03:57,780
We have.

103
00:03:57,970 --> 00:03:59,110
Now let's get into the heart of it.

104
00:03:59,350 --> 00:03:59,550
All right.

105
00:03:59,870 --> 00:04:01,170
The questions themselves.

106
00:04:01,190 --> 00:04:02,450
The dreaded questions.

107
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:04,890
The guide breaks them down into three categories.

108
00:04:04,910 --> 00:04:05,550
Okay.

109
00:04:05,570 --> 00:04:07,540
General, behavioral, and role-specific.

110
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:09,100
Makes sense.

111
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,640
Let's start with the one that I think trips up a lot of people.

112
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:12,300
Okay.

113
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:13,060
Tell me about yourself.

114
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:13,900
Oh, yeah.

115
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:15,230
This one seems so simple.

116
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:16,210
It does.

117
00:04:16,810 --> 00:04:18,890
But it can be surprisingly tricky.

118
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:19,410
It is.

119
00:04:19,430 --> 00:04:20,430
I always get flustered.

120
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:24,300
I think the key here is to remember that it's not about reciting your resume.

121
00:04:24,460 --> 00:04:24,660
Right.

122
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:33,790
It's about telling a story, a concise and compelling story that connects your experiences directly to what this particular company needs.

123
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:38,460
So it's about highlighting the skills and experiences that are most relevant to the job.

124
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:39,020
Exactly.

125
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:45,200
And framing them in a way that shows how your past successes can translate into future wins for them.

126
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:46,830
So it's almost like a mini sales pitch.

127
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:47,290
Yeah.

128
00:04:47,310 --> 00:04:50,450
I like that a mini sales pitch tailored to that specific customer.

129
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:50,810
Okay.

130
00:04:50,830 --> 00:04:51,330
That makes sense.

131
00:04:51,350 --> 00:04:52,770
You're selling yourself and your skills.

132
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:53,090
All right.

133
00:04:53,110 --> 00:04:53,890
So tell me about yourself.

134
00:04:53,910 --> 00:04:54,630
We've got that one down.

135
00:04:54,770 --> 00:04:55,100
We do.

136
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:56,740
Let's move on to behavioral questions.

137
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:57,220
Okay.

138
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:02,920
These are the ones that ask you to describe a specific situation and how you handled it.

139
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:03,240
Right.

140
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:06,950
Like describe a challenge you faced at work and how you overcame it.

141
00:05:06,970 --> 00:05:07,770
Oh, yes.

142
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:09,690
Those always make me nervous.

143
00:05:09,750 --> 00:05:17,150
I know they can be a bit daunting, but they're actually a great opportunity to showcase your problem-solving skills and your ability to stay positive under pressure.

144
00:05:17,810 --> 00:05:20,740
Okay, so how do you approach these types of questions?

145
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:23,700
Well, there's a really helpful framework called the STAR method.

146
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:25,130
Okay, I've heard of that.

147
00:05:25,510 --> 00:05:25,890
Have you?

148
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:26,970
Yeah, but remind me.

149
00:05:27,290 --> 00:05:28,570
Yeah, so it stands for

150
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:31,200
Situation, task, action, result.

151
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:31,440
Right.

152
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:35,180
So you start by setting the scene, describing the situation you're in.

153
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:42,290
Then you outline the task you were responsible for, the actions you took to address the challenge, and finally, the results you achieved.

154
00:05:42,630 --> 00:05:47,000
So it's all about telling a clear and concise story with a beginning, middle, and end.

155
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:47,940
Exactly.

156
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:50,180
And emphasizing the positive outcome.

157
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:55,690
So even if the situation itself was challenging, you want to focus on how you learned and grew from it.

158
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:56,560
Absolutely.

159
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:59,380
You know, show that you're able to turn obstacles into opportunities.

160
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:00,380
Okay, that makes sense.

161
00:06:01,050 --> 00:06:05,410
And you mentioned struggling with these, so I would say, you know, practice with a few work examples beforehand.

162
00:06:05,570 --> 00:06:06,940
Yeah, I think I need to do that.

163
00:06:06,950 --> 00:06:09,140
Yeah, pick ones that really showcase your strengths.

164
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:14,210
You know, your problem-solving skills, your leadership, your ability to work under pressure.

165
00:06:15,010 --> 00:06:15,590
Great advice.

166
00:06:15,730 --> 00:06:16,110
Thanks.

167
00:06:17,510 --> 00:06:20,500
OK, so we've covered general questions and behavioral questions.

168
00:06:20,630 --> 00:06:21,340
We have.

169
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:23,920
Now let's move on to role specific questions.

170
00:06:23,980 --> 00:06:24,480
All right.

171
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:30,120
These are the ones that really dig into your knowledge and experience in a particular field.

172
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:30,560
Exactly.

173
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:34,390
And they're designed to see if you've gone beyond just reading the job description.

174
00:06:34,970 --> 00:06:39,100
So it's about showing that you've really thought about the role and the challenges you might face.

175
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:39,860
Absolutely.

176
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:46,110
The guide gave an example about teaching positions, asking about your teaching philosophy and classroom management style.

177
00:06:46,270 --> 00:06:48,790
Yeah, those are really common questions for educators.

178
00:06:48,910 --> 00:06:49,130
Right.

179
00:06:49,180 --> 00:06:54,280
So it's about anticipating the types of questions they might ask and having thoughtful answers prepared.

180
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:54,960
Exactly.

181
00:06:54,980 --> 00:07:02,630
If you can demonstrate that you've already thought about these things, it shows you're genuinely interested and you're already thinking like a member of the team.

182
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:03,780
So preparation is key.

183
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:04,400
Always.

184
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:09,240
Now, we've talked a lot about answering their questions, but what about the part where we get to ask questions?

185
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:10,880
Yes, this is so important.

186
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:11,300
It is.

187
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:15,890
The guide makes a good point about using this time to gather information.

188
00:07:16,230 --> 00:07:18,330
It's not just about them grilling us.

189
00:07:18,610 --> 00:07:18,810
Right.

190
00:07:18,890 --> 00:07:22,150
It's a chance for us to learn more about the company, the team, the role.

191
00:07:22,210 --> 00:07:22,710
Exactly.

192
00:07:22,810 --> 00:07:29,240
And asking insightful questions shows that you're engaged and curious, which are qualities that employers love.

193
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:31,980
So what are some examples of good questions to ask?

194
00:07:32,050 --> 00:07:36,670
Well, you could ask something like, what are the biggest opportunities facing this team right now?

195
00:07:37,610 --> 00:07:38,210
That's a good one.

196
00:07:38,490 --> 00:07:41,660
Or what's one thing you wish you had known before joining this company?

197
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:42,560
Oh, I like that.

198
00:07:42,860 --> 00:07:43,040
Yeah.

199
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:52,050
Those types of questions, they get you real insights into the company culture, the team dynamic, and even the potential for growth within the role.

200
00:07:52,070 --> 00:07:53,560
It's about going beyond the surface level.

201
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:54,040
Exactly.

202
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:56,180
Showing that you're thinking long term.

203
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:56,440
Right.

204
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:57,580
So we've covered the questions.

205
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:58,200
We have.

206
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:02,890
But there's another aspect of the interview that's so important, and that's professional presentation.

207
00:08:03,830 --> 00:08:04,070
Right.

208
00:08:04,430 --> 00:08:05,420
First impressions matter.

209
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:06,220
They do.

210
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:09,820
And I think this goes beyond just wearing the right outfit.

211
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:10,460
Oh, absolutely.

212
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:12,330
It's about projecting confidence.

213
00:08:12,570 --> 00:08:17,230
OK. You know, standing tall, making eye contact, offering a firm handshake.

214
00:08:17,750 --> 00:08:21,120
Those nonverbal cues, they speak volumes.

215
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,230
So it's about being aware of your body language.

216
00:08:23,310 --> 00:08:23,850
Exactly.

217
00:08:23,910 --> 00:08:28,830
And while authenticity is key, you should also research the company culture.

218
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:31,570
Right, because different industries have different expectations.

219
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:32,140
Exactly.

220
00:08:32,220 --> 00:08:35,580
A tech startup might be more casual than a corporate law firm.

221
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:39,890
So it's about finding that balance between being yourself and reading the room.

222
00:08:40,130 --> 00:08:40,550
Exactly.

223
00:08:40,650 --> 00:08:45,000
You want to show them that you can fit in, but you also don't want to pretend to be someone you're not.

224
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:46,660
And what about communication skills?

225
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:47,300
Yeah.

226
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:51,450
Specifically for those who might be interviewing in their non-native language.

227
00:08:51,710 --> 00:08:52,470
That's a great point.

228
00:08:52,530 --> 00:08:53,600
Practice is crucial.

229
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:58,100
You know, rehearse your answers out loud, focusing on clarity and pronunciation.

230
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:05,010
And there are also great resources out there like Toastmasters or even interview coaching specifically designed for non-native speakers.

231
00:09:05,090 --> 00:09:06,770
Yeah, those are fantastic resources.

232
00:09:06,790 --> 00:09:11,440
Yeah, they can really help you build confidence and refine your communication skills.

233
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:11,760
All right.

234
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:15,790
One last thing that really emphasizes the importance of mock interviews.

235
00:09:16,190 --> 00:09:16,690
Oh, yes.

236
00:09:16,850 --> 00:09:18,990
Mock interviews are so valuable.

237
00:09:19,050 --> 00:09:20,190
I know they can be nerve wracking.

238
00:09:20,410 --> 00:09:21,330
Yeah, I get it.

239
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:23,840
What are your thoughts on like why they're so beneficial?

240
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:27,240
Well, think of it like a scrimmage before the big game.

241
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:30,300
OK. You know, you get to test your plays, work out.

242
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:32,870
The kinks get comfortable in that game environment.

243
00:09:33,070 --> 00:09:34,230
It's about getting those reps in.

244
00:09:34,270 --> 00:09:34,790
Exactly.

245
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:38,110
And the feedback you get from mock interviews is gold.

246
00:09:38,550 --> 00:09:38,740
Right.

247
00:09:39,030 --> 00:09:40,740
Even if it's just from a friend or family member.

248
00:09:40,770 --> 00:09:41,450
Absolutely.

249
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:45,790
Even having someone run through some questions with you can make a huge difference.

250
00:09:45,890 --> 00:09:48,160
It helps you identify areas where you can improve.

251
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:48,860
Exactly.

252
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:50,560
And it boosts your overall confidence.

253
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:52,420
So much good stuff here.

254
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:53,080
There is.

255
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:58,270
As we wrap up this deep dive, what's the biggest takeaway you want our listener to walk away with?

256
00:09:58,750 --> 00:10:01,840
I think a job interview is a two-way street.

257
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:04,920
It's not just about proving yourself worthy of the job.

258
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:08,870
It's about deciding if the job and the company are worthy of you.

259
00:10:09,350 --> 00:10:10,490
That's a really great point.

260
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:15,300
You know, thorough preparation empowers you to make that decision with confidence.

261
