469
00:19:15,910 --> 00:19:17,910
Like, tell me about a time you failed.

470
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:19,740
Oh, man, those are tough.

471
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:21,780
Yeah, they can really throw you off balance.

472
00:19:21,900 --> 00:19:25,130
Because you're trying to present yourself in the best light, and then they hit you with that.

473
00:19:25,350 --> 00:19:25,610
Right.

474
00:19:26,210 --> 00:19:28,710
But the key here is to reframe it.

475
00:19:28,930 --> 00:19:33,380
See it as an opportunity to show how you handle challenges, how you learn from mistakes.

476
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:35,640
So it's not about pretending you're perfect.

477
00:19:35,660 --> 00:19:36,180
Not at all.

478
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:37,940
Everyone makes mistakes.

479
00:19:38,270 --> 00:19:40,250
It's what you do afterwards that matters.

480
00:19:40,670 --> 00:19:42,570
So how do you actually answer that kind of question?

481
00:19:42,610 --> 00:19:43,490
What's the approach?

482
00:19:43,830 --> 00:19:45,930
Well, there's a structure called the STAR method.

483
00:19:46,210 --> 00:19:49,250
It stands for Situation, Task, Action, Result.

484
00:19:49,580 --> 00:19:50,420
Okay, I've heard of that.

485
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:52,120
Yeah, it's a really helpful framework.

486
00:19:52,800 --> 00:20:02,290
So you briefly describe the situation, outline your specific task, detail the actions you took, and then highlight the positive outcome, even if it wasn't a perfect success.

487
00:20:02,510 --> 00:20:06,090
So you're showing that you can analyze, adapt, and grow from challenges.

488
00:20:06,310 --> 00:20:09,950
Exactly, and that you're not afraid to own up to your mistakes.

489
00:20:10,070 --> 00:20:12,290
Which is a valuable quality in any employee.

490
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:17,380
Okay, so we've talked about showcasing experience, handling tough questions.

491
00:20:18,070 --> 00:20:20,990
